# Media Lifecycle Invariants — Read Before "Simplifying"

Each item below came out of a full-app audit that fixed a real bug. In every case the
*pre-fix* version looks like the simpler, more obvious code, so these are exactly the
lines a future cleanup pass tends to revert. Keep the behaviour unless the user asks
for a change and names the item.

## Warm web views are capped and destroyed, not just dropped

`WarmWebSessionPool` enforces `maxWebViews` and calls `WarmWebSession.destroy()` on
eviction. Each warm page is a real web content process; releasing the reference without
tearing down the `WKWebView`, its message handler, and its delegates leaks the process.

Launch does **not** warm websites. Show website cards warm via `warmSoon(_:)` when a
Show opens; bookmarks warm one at a time as they scroll into view, spaced and
watchdogged. Do not reintroduce a launch `warmAll()` that warms every bookmark up
front — that competed with the first frame and made the whole app sluggish at launch.

## Ambient audio pauses, it never stops

`AudioAmbientPolicy.applyYieldIfNeeded(for:)` / `forWebMedia:` call `pause()` (with fade)
when Settings → Pause Music for Video is on. `stop()` tears down the queue and mini
player, so yielding to audible video or website media would silently discard the user's
playback state. Pausing is the point, not an oversight. Default is off: music keeps
playing under video. Muted library video and silent web pages do not yield even when
the preference is on. Audible presentation uses `.moviePlayback` only when yielding;
otherwise it stays on `.default` so the mix is not ducked.

## Photo capture has no fallback

`capturePhotoToLibrary` uses only the full-resolution frame it renders on demand and
reports `CaptureError.noFrame` otherwise. Do not "improve" it by falling back to
`latestSampleImage` or `lastFrame`: both are downscaled to `tileStillMaxEdge`, and
`lastFrame` is persisted and can outlive the session that produced it, so a fallback
writes a stale thumbnail into the user's photo library instead of failing honestly.

Related: `latestSampleImage` must keep exactly one writer (the throttled tile path) so
it stays tile-sized. A second writer storing full-resolution frames is what made the
above bug possible.

## Grid layout floors its tile dimensions

`LibraryGridViewController+HomeLayout` routes tile sizing through `columnWidth(...)`,
which floors the result at `minimumTileSide`. `NSCollectionLayoutDimension.absolute`
throws on a non-positive value, and the container width is briefly zero during early
layout, so the `max(...)` is a crash guard rather than defensive noise.

## Sync only reports success it can back up

`iPhoneConnectionManager.replayLibrary` counts per-item send failures and calls
`completion(false)` unless every item transferred. Reporting unconditional success made
`MultiTVSyncCoordinator` mark a replica caught up when it wasn't, which suppressed the
retry. Similarly, `pendingRestoreId` clears only *after* a successful send, and
cancelling a transfer must also cancel the replica fan-out.

## Unreadable saved pages are preserved, not overwritten

`WebPageStore.load()` salvages entries individually and, when it still can't decode,
copies the raw bytes to a backup key and starts empty. It must never write over data it
failed to read — that turns a decode bug into permanent loss of the user's bookmarks.

## tvOS: one teardown path for the video player

Stopping playback goes through `ImageViewController.retireCurrentPlayer(...)`. Ad-hoc
teardown is how videos ended up still playing audio after a transition to an image or
the grid, with companions left showing a stale playing state.

## tvOS: granular data-source updates do not also reload

`MediaDataSource`'s add/remove/move notify only their specific delegate callback. They
must not also call `mediaDataDidChange()`; pairing a batch update with a full
`reloadData()` stomps the animation and can crash. Selection is re-validated in the
batch-update completion instead.

---

If one of these genuinely needs to change, STOP and ask the user first, naming the item.
